# skill: quick_sort

## Contract

Sorts an integer array in ascending order using the QuickSort algorithm.

**Signature:**
```
void quick_sort(int arr[], int low, int high)
```

**Parameters:**
- `arr[]` — integer array to sort (modified in-place)
- `low` — starting index (inclusive)
- `high` — ending index (inclusive)

**Behavior:**
- Partitions array around a pivot (last element)
- Recursively sorts left and right subarrays
- Uses explicit stack-based recursion to stay within static memory bounds

**Complexity:**
- Average: O(n log n)
- Worst: O(n²) when array is already sorted or reverse sorted
- Space: O(log n) stack depth

**Stability:** Not stable

## Dependencies

- `array-base` — array subscript and length conventions
- `comparison` — `compare_gt(a, b)` predicate
- `signal-handler` — error/signal conventions
- `printf-basic` — debug output if needed

## Slots

| Slot | Type | Description |
|------|------|-------------|
| `pivot` | `int` | Chosen as `arr[high]` (last element) |
| `i` | `int` | Partition boundary index |
| `pi` | `int` | Final pivot index after partition |

## Boundaries

- `low >= high` — base case: subarray has 0 or 1 element, return immediately
- Pivot selection is deterministic (last element) — vulnerable to worst-case O(n²) on sorted input
- No bounds checking on `arr[]` — caller must ensure valid range

## Examples

```
quick_sort(arr, 0, 4);  // sort arr[0..4]
quick_sort(arr, 0, n-1); // sort entire array
```

[code]
```c
// Quick sort — O(n log n) average, O(n²) worst, not stable
// Uses stack for explicit recursion to stay within static memory
void quick_sort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    if (low >= high) return;

    int pivot = arr[high];
    int i = low - 1;

    for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
        if (compare_gt(pivot, arr[j])) {
            i++;
            swap(&arr[i], &arr[j]);
        }
    }
    swap(&arr[i + 1], &arr[high]);

    int pi = i + 1;
    quick_sort(arr, low, pi - 1);
    quick_sort(arr, pi + 1, high);
}
```
